<!--problem definition of this work -->
To enable the practical application of roles, two major requirements have to be fulfilled.
First, besides the intuitive semantics underlying the role concept, its notions must be formally specified to create a coherent understanding of relational and context-dependent roles.
Second, to permit scalability of role-based modeling languages automatic mechanisms to validate the well-formedness and consistency are required.
Therefore, a formal model is indispensable.
In sum, a major blocking factor for the practical application of roles
is their lack of a consistent formal model. 
Thus, this paper provides a formal model for a role-based modeling language
supporting both natures of roles.
This allows for the formal and automatic verification of 
well-formedness, compliance, and validity of models at design time and their instances at runtime.

